# Wave-2 falsify items (exec'd by make-spec.py w2; uses add(), REV (= the AMENDMENT revision the cohort
# reads), FREEZE, L, W, WD, SC, BASE, ENTRY, CEN, LINT, C1C, C2C, C2S, C3C, PROV, REG, HASH).
W1 = f'{W}/w1'

# ---- re-run every counting command at the amendment revision (3 executors) ----
add('R51ExecRunC1', 'executor', ['depth', 'C1', 'rerun'], 're-run the C1 counting commands', f"""
Item W2-R1. Re-run claim C1's instrument at the amendment revision {REV} from a fresh clone (`D=$(mktemp -d {SC}/R51ExecRunC1/fx.XXXXXX); git clone -q --shared {L} "$D"; git -C "$D" checkout -q --detach {REV}`; verify toplevel): (a) `python3 tools/lint-grounding-r51.py --revision {REV}` (expect lint_ok 3, rc 0); (b) `python3 tools/lint-grounding-r51.py --revision {REV} --emit-gaps` and diff with the entry's `### Range gaps (R51)` block; (c) `python3 tools/check-scrub-admission-r51.py --revision {REV} --no-base-check --section-hash`; (d) the counter with no cohort (`--no-base-check`; expect `lint_ok 3 quotes_ok 3 seats_ok 0 verdicts_ok 0 marks_ok 0`); (e) the same at the base {BASE}. Then read what the wave-1 pre-read seats found ({W1}/R51W1Pre*.md, first line VERDICT) and, for each reported defect, say whether the amendment at {REV} fixed it (`git -C {L} diff {FREEZE} {REV} -- {ENTRY}`): a table of (seat, defect, fixed yes/no/disputed). Report exact outputs and exit codes.""")
add('R51ExecRunC2', 'executor', ['operability', 'C2', 'rerun'], 're-run the C2 counting command', f"""
Item W2-R2. Re-run claim C2's counting command at {REV}: `python3 {L}/{C2S} --revision {REV} --scratch {SC}/R51ExecRunC2/s` (3-10 min; 1800 s timeout; the default is `--runs 1` for the scorer's counter invocations) and report the one-line JSON (every leg of `detail`) and the exit code. Then run the counter itself the way the SCORECARD cites it, with the default two runs: `python3 {L}/{C2C} --revision {REV} --scratch {SC}/R51ExecRunC2/c` and report `idiom_sites base_misses full_misses cases pass_runs case_ok mut_applied mut_caught` and rc. Do not edit the census. Note `cat /proc/loadavg` at each start.""")
add('R51ExecRunC3', 'executor', ['adjacency', 'C3', 'rerun'], 're-run the C3 counting command', f"""
Item W2-R3. Re-run claim C3's counting command at {REV} from a fresh clone: `python3 tools/check-adjacency-table-r51.py --revision {REV} --lane "$D"` and from the lane `python3 {L}/{C3C} --revision {REV}`; report both one-line JSONs (every leg) and rc, and the live-tip receipts of any sibling that moved since the freeze (`git -C /home/vgpnk/Projects/shokunin/<sib>-shokunin rev-parse HEAD` vs the registry pin). Then read the wave-1 C3 attack reports ({W1}/R51CritCheatC3a.md, R51CritCheatC3b.md, R51RevC3.md, R51MutC3a.md, R51MutC3b.md) and for each hole they report say whether it still reproduces at {REV} (re-run the reporter's own reproducer): a table (seat, hole, reproduces yes/no).""")

# ---- attack the amendment diff and the lesson/report text (6) ----
add('R51RevAmendA', 'reviewer', ['depth', 'C1', 'amendment'], 'review the entry amendment A', f"""
Item W2-A1. Review the pre-w2 amendment of the entry: `git -C {L} diff {FREEZE} {REV} -- {ENTRY}`. For EVERY changed or added line decide: is it grounded in a pinned source line you opened (cite file:line), is it narrower than or equal to what the source supports, and does it keep the row's other clauses true? A line that adds a new claim must carry its own quote or citation. Also compare the diff with the wave-1 pre-read findings in {W1}/R51W1Pre*.md: any finding the amendment did NOT take, and any change the findings did NOT ask for (an unexplained edit is a defect). Report each changed line with a verdict.""")
add('R51RevAmendB', 'reviewer', ['depth', 'C1', 'amendment'], 'review the entry amendment B', f"""
Item W2-A2. Second independent review of the amendment diff (`git -C {L} diff {FREEZE} {REV} -- {ENTRY}`), but from the READER's side: print the three rows at {REV} (`git -C {L} show {REV}:{ENTRY}`, sections GR1-GR3 and Limits) and read them without the diff. Find any sentence a reader could take as a claim the pinned code does not decide, any word like first/only/always without a decided source, and any `[inference]` mark that has become stale (marks the amendment should have added or dropped). Cite pinned lines for each. Do not read the counted cohort's reports.""")
add('R51CritAmend', 'critic', ['depth', 'C1', 'amendment'], 'attack the entry amendment', f"""
Item W2-A3. Attack the amendment and the `### Range gaps (R51)` block: (a) are there citations in the rows that the lint's tokenizer cannot see, that lie outside the declared ranges (read every sentence of GR1-GR3 at {REV}; list each `file:line`-like reference including prose such as 'line 3159' or 'lines 1171 to 1195')? (b) does the gap block name a line that no row cites (bloat) or omit one (hole)? run `python3 {L}/{LINT} --revision {REV}` and `--emit-gaps` and diff; (c) does the Limits section still carry any stale statement the rewrite made false (`git -C {L} show {REV}:{ENTRY}`, the bullets of `## Limits` and the `## Gate status` sections: cancel/suspend clauses, `by SR7`, 'argument copy')? (d) is the `source:` header line and QUALITY item 7 consistent with the sources the rows now cite (S240 for the ioctl clauses)? Report each defect with the exact replacement.""")
add('R51RevLessonDraft', 'reviewer', ['calibration', 'lesson'], 'review the lesson draft', f"""
Item W2-A4. Review the L1's lesson draft at {L}/.ufo/waves/round-51/lesson-draft.md (committed with the amendment; if absent, say so and review `git -C {L} show {REV}:corpus/lessons/round-51.md`). For every Gate row: re-open the source (URL in the row) and confirm the quote is verbatim, <=25 words, with the stated line; for every Mistakes row: confirm the evidence pointer resolves (`git -C {L} show {REV}:<path>` or an existing file) and that 'captured-by' names a rule or criterion that exists (grep corpus/QUALITY.md at {REV}); for the scoreboard (if present): spot-check eight cells against the cited seat reports. Report each wrong cell with the correction.""")
add('R51CritMistakes', 'critic', ['calibration', 'lesson'], 'attack the mistakes mining', f"""
Item W2-A5. The round prompt requires 'mine one mistake' with a fix that 'counts only once shown able to fail'. Read the draft Mistakes table ({L}/.ufo/waves/round-51/lesson-draft.md) and ask, per row: is the stated fix shown able to fail (a control that FAILs before the fix and PASSes after, with a pointer), or only asserted? For the misroot row: what rule would catch an `edit` on a bare filename next time, and is it in a committed place (QUALITY.md or SKILL.md)? Search the round-50 and round-49 reports and spot-checks for a mistake class the draft MISSED (the round-50 closure's routed defects: {L}/corpus/lessons/round-50-verifier-spotcheck.md §6). Propose the exact row text for each omission.""")
add('R51RevScoreboard', 'reviewer', ['calibration', 'considerthese'], 'verify the counter-attack scoreboard', f"""
Item W2-A6. The L1 landed (or will land) a counter-attack scoreboard from the considerthese proposal (`{L}/considerthese/2026-10-26.md`, second bullet under Self-improvement ideas; the mined table is {W1}/R51ScoutConsider.md; the L1's draft is in {L}/.ufo/waves/round-51/lesson-draft.md). Verify: every row cites a seat report or battery receipt that EXISTS (`ls`), each arm count equals the number of attack seat reports you can enumerate for that counter and round, and each 'survived' cell is backed by that report's own words. List unsourced or miscounted cells with corrections. Say whether the proposal's question ('converging or moving the hole?') is answered by the data.""")

# ---- gate mutation, final cheats, sources verification (9) ----
add('R51MutBattery', 'mutation-tester', ['depth', 'C1', 'mutation'], 'is the battery sufficient: surviving mutants from wave 1', f"""
Item W2-M1. Wave 1 ran lint/counter mutants ({W1}/R51MutLintA.md, R51MutLintB.md, R51MutC2.md, R51MutC3a.md, R51MutC3b.md). Read them and list every SURVIVING mutant (a mutant the committed batteries did not kill). For each survivor decide whether it is an equivalent mutant or a control gap; for control gaps write the exact K control that would kill it (fixture + expected verdict) and run it against the original and the mutant in scratch to prove it can FAIL. Report a table (mutant, survived?, proposed control, original verdict, mutant verdict). Do not edit the lane's battery; the L1 decides which controls to add as a post-measurement note (the counters themselves are frozen).""")
add('R51CritFinalC1', 'critic', ['depth', 'C1', 'cheat'], 'final cheat attempt on C1', f"""
Item W2-C1. Last attempt to pass C1's counter WITHOUT the counted cohort reading the rows: using only files you write in scratch, try to make `python3 {L}/{C1C} --revision {REV} --no-base-check --entry-file ... --wave-dir ... --results-file ... --dispatch-file ...` print PASS on the REAL entry bytes at {REV}: forge 18 reports + a results file + a dispatch file. Report how many lines of effort it took and what a forger needs to know (the section hashes are printed by `--section-hash`; the report format is in the counter's docstring). Then say what independent check, if any, a reviewer should run on a PASS to be sure the 18 reports were written by seats (name concrete evidence: `w2-results.json` entries' ids vs the dispatch registry; the agent ids in `{W}/w2-dispatch.json`; file mtimes vs wave start). Read-only on the lane; scratch only.""")
add('R51CritFinalC23', 'critic', ['operability', 'C2', 'cheat'], 'final cheat attempt on C2 and C3 together', f"""
Item W2-C2. Look at what the wave-1 attackers found ({W1}/R51CritCheatC2a.md, R51CritCheatC2b.md, R51CritCheatC3a.md, R51CritCheatC3b.md, R51RevC2.md, R51RevC3.md) and re-run each reported hole at {REV} (the counters are frozen: the holes that remain are the reported limits). For each hole decide whether it falls INSIDE the claim's stated scope (the four cheat classes for C2; displayed-vs-registry binding and override for C3) or is a named limit. Report a table (hole, in scope?, still reproduces?, evidence). If a hole is in scope and reproduces, say so plainly: it makes the claim FAIL honestly rather than get narrowed.""")
for sid, label in (('S237', 'btrfs-progs scrub.c'), ('S242', 'kernel scrub.c'), ('S247', 'kernel dev-replace.c'), ('S256', 'GNU grep(1)')):
    add(f'R51ScoutVerify{sid}', 'scout', ['freshness', 'frontier', 'verify'], f'independent re-open of {sid}', f"""
Item W2-S ({sid}, {label}). A different seat re-opened {sid} in wave 1 ({W1}/R51ScoutReopen{sid}.md, or R51ScoutS256.md for S256). Do NOT read that report first. Re-open the source yourself from the URL in the row (`git -C {L} show {REV}:corpus/SOURCES.md`, grep `^| {sid} |`), record access time, size and sha256, and compare with the pin in the row; locate the quote the lesson's Gate block gives for {sid} ({L}/.ufo/waves/round-51/lesson-draft.md) in the live bytes with its line number. Then read the wave-1 report and say whether you AGREE on each of: byte-identical, quote verbatim and <=25 words, line number. Disagreement is a defect of the draft; say which of the two is right and why.""")
add('R51ScoutNewRow', 'scout', ['freshness', 'frontier', 'verify'], 'verify a proposed new SOURCES row', f"""
Item W2-S5. The wave-1 scout `R51ScoutS256` ({W1}/R51ScoutS256.md) proposed a candidate new row for the Bash manual's `pipefail` text (id S257). Open https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Pipelines.html yourself: record access time, size, sha256 and the located quote (<=25 words, verbatim, with its position); check the 11 cells of the proposed row (no literal `|` in a cell, valid ISO `opened` and symbolic `recheck`, `used-by` resolves), check that the URL is not already in {L}/corpus/SOURCES.md (a duplicate URL is a census W04/E04 finding): `grep -c 'node/Pipelines' {L}/corpus/SOURCES.md`. Then in a scratch copy of the lane's corpus (`cp -r` only corpus/SOURCES.md plus the files the census needs, or run with `--file` on a copy; see `/home/vgpnk/Projects/shokunin/scripts/sources-census.sh validate --file <path>`), append the row and run the census: exit code and findings. Report ACCEPT or REFUSE with the reason.""")

# ---- no-harm, scope, host operability, cross-claim (5) ----
add('R51RevNoHarmFinal', 'reviewer', ['calibration', 'no-harm'], 'no-harm at the amendment', f"""
Item W2-N1. No-harm at {REV}: run `python3 {L}/tools/check-self-assessment.py --revision {REV}` (the L1 restates the SCORECARD axes at the end; report the mismatches NOW so the restatement is complete), `python3 {L}/tools/check-registry-coverage.py --revision {REV}` (expect missing 0), `python3 {L}/tools/check-adjacency-anchors.py --revision {REV}`, `python3 {L}/tools/check-fd-band-r49.py --revision {REV}` and `bash {L}/{CEN} --selftest` (one selftest-bearing command at a time). Compare with {W}/before/self-assessment-at-base.json and registry-coverage-at-base.json. Say which axis, if any, the round harms (an instrument that now FAILs a previously passing check is harm; a new FAIL of a dormant gate is not).""")
add('R51ExecLiveHost', 'executor', ['operability', 'host', 'live'], 'live host census for the report', f"""
Item W2-N2. Host operability evidence for the report. Run, read-only and in this order: `bash {L}/{CEN}` (live census; capture the full output and exit code; the verdict was DEGRADED rc 1 at the round start: packages, drivers, journal, update-safety, oom), `bash {L}/{CEN} --identity`, `bash {L}/tools/boot-chain-audit.sh --selftest` (expect PASS total=49 failed=0) and `systemctl --user is-system-running`, `systemctl --user --failed --no-legend | wc -l`, `pacman -Qk 2>/dev/null | tail -1` (read-only), `df -h / /boot /fast | tail -3`. Compare each plane's verdict with the round-start capture in {W}/before/ (there is no census capture there: say 'no round-start capture' and use the SCORECARD round-50 text instead) and report any plane that changed. Never run an install, an upgrade or any mutating command. Copy outputs (not raw journal bodies) into {WD}/R51ExecLiveHost/.""")
add('R51SentFinalScope', 'sentinel', ['calibration', 'scope'], 'final scope and misroot sweep', f"""
Item W2-N3. Sentinel pass at {REV}, as in the wave-1 scope item but now over the WHOLE round: `git -C {L} diff {BASE} {REV} --name-status` (paths in scope; no MANIFEST/fleet templates/scripts/.ufo/state), `git -C /home/vgpnk/Projects/shokunin status --porcelain` for any untracked/modified file whose content names tsukuroi or round 51 at the fleet root (report the path; do not touch), scratch leftovers (`ls {SC}` — every seat's dir must be gone: list survivors; they are defects of that seat), and any seat report outside `{W}/w1/` or `{W}/w2/` (`find {L} -newer {W}/frozen/FROZEN.md -type f -not -path '*/.git/*' -not -path '*/.jj/*' | head -200` — list files not under the two wave dirs and not the L1's own commits).""")
add('R51ConnFinal', 'connector', ['calibration', 'cross-claim'], 'cross-claim pattern across waves', f"""
Item W2-N4. Connector seat: read every wave-1 seat report's first line ({W1}/*.md; there are ~54) and the wave-2 reports that exist when you start, and find the cross-cutting patterns the individual seats cannot see: the same defect class reported by several seats under different names; a hole one seat reports that another seat's control would have caught; a claim one seat's evidence contradicts another's. Deliver a table of (pattern, seats, what it means for the claims) and at most five recommendations for the L1's report — one line each. Do not re-run anything.""")
add('R51ScribeReportClaims', 'scribe', ['calibration', 'report'], 'draft the before/after text for the report', f"""
Item W2-N5 (prose only). From {W}/before/*.json and {W}/frozen/FROZEN.md draft the 'Before → after measurements' section skeleton for the round report: one entry per claim with the counting command, the before value (exact JSON legs from the before receipts), and `AFTER: PENDING w2` for the legs the cohort or the final re-run decides; add the sentence each instrument claim (C2, C3) must carry that its measured bytes are the counters and controls, not a change to the census, PROVINCE.md or the registry. Keep it under 60 lines.""")
